    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601310": {
      "title": "Kitchen Assistant",
      "description": "Job Description - Kitchen Assistant (2601310) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Kitchen Assistant (2601310) Kitchen Assistant - 2601310 __________ CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES | TE URU KAHIKA UNIVERSITY UNION | TE UNIANA O ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA About our team/Mō te tīma College Catering is one of New Zealand's largest institutional food service operations, producing more than 8,500 meals each day across eleven University Colleges. Our team takes pride in providing quality food and service that supports the student experience and contributes to Otago's vibrant collegiate community. The role/Te mahi We are seeking a reliable and motivated Kitchen Assistant to join our busy Central Production Kitchen team. Working in a fast-paced and supportive environment, you'll play an important role in helping deliver high-quality meals to thousands of students each day. Key responsibilities •    Washing dishes, trays, utensils, and kitchen equipment. •    Maintaining a clean, safe, and organised kitchen environment. •    Assisting with basic food preparation as required. •    Supporting chefs and kitchen staff with daily tasks. •    Following food safety and health and safety procedures. •    Receiving and storing deliveries. •    Assisting with end-of-day cleaning and shutdown procedures. What we offer •    Stable, permanent employment within a well-established organisation. •    A supportive and inclusive team environment. •    Opportunities to develop food service and hospitality skills. •    Semester-time working arrangements. Further details/Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time, semester-time-only position based in Dunedin, working during the academic year (February to November). Hours of work are 40 hours per week, worked on a rostered basis. The successful candidate will commence in September 2026. The appointment range is $26.43 to $27.48 per hour. While this position will initially be based in the Central Production Kitchen, it may be relocated to another University College on the Dunedin campus to meet operational requirements. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact Executive Chef Charlie Mancey via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including a CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference 2601310 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be invited to interview before the closing date. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. #LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Charlie Mancey Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Assistant - Outlet/Kitchen/Cafe/Retail/Stores (Range of Appointments $26.43 to $27.48 per hour, Top of Range $27.48) Organisation Catering Job Function Food and Hospitality Services ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601260": {
      "title": "Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)",
      "description": "Job Description - Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)  (2601260) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)  (2601260) Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer) - 2601260 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE | TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI Are you an enthusiastic motivated Sonographer wishing to work in the area of vascular disease? Then this job is for you. We invite you to apply for this full-time position of Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer) in the Otago Vascular Diagnostics Assessment Unit within the Department of Surgery and Critical Care. About our team/Mō te tīma We are a team of vascular surgeons, technologists, vascular sonographers and research staff using various diagnostic investigations to research the causes and management of vascular conditions. Our unit provides clinical diagnostic services to the Southern District Health Board, and to the wider community, to assist in the diagnosis and treatment of vascular diseases. The unit is also part of the Vascular Research Group and works closely with other research groups at local, national and international levels. The role/Te mahi In this role you will join a multidisciplinary focused clinical research team investigating vascular disease. This position involves working within a fun team of vascular technicians and sonographers in assessing patients with vascular disease, using physiological techniques and ultrasound. Assessments are carried out for the purpose of both clinical patient care and research. The successful applicant would learn the specific techniques and assist in the general tasks of the Assessment Unit. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate would be expected and supported to support clinical research and teaching, along with clinical ultrasound services to Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora. You will have: •    A post graduate diploma in medical ultrasound (DMU) or an equivalent recognised qualification (We may consider a new graduate with specific vascular training). •    To be registrable with the Medical Radiation Technologists Board (MRTB). •    Good organisational skills. •    A professional approach and ability to relate well when dealing with the public. •    High standards of both oral and written communication skills. Further details/Pūroko This is a permanent, full time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Dunedin, commencing in October 2026. Part-time hours may also be considered. The salary range for this position is $71,630 to $110,000 per annum. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information please contact Brigid G. Hill via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference 2601260 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026 Applications may be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Ms Brigid (Gerry) Hill Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Contact Department for Information Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600844": {
      "title": "Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer",
      "description": "Job Description - Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer (2600844) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer (2600844) Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer - 2600844 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE | TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI Are you an enthusiastic motivated person wishing to work in the area of vascular disease? Then this job is for you. We invite you to apply for this full-time position of Vascular Laboratory Technician in the Otago Vascular Diagnostics Unit within the Section of Surgery. About our team/Mō te tīma We are a team of vascular surgeons, technologists and research staff using various diagnostic investigations to research the causes and management of vascular conditions. Our unit provides clinical diagnostic services to the Te Whatu Ora - Southern, and to the wider community, to assist in the diagnosis and treatment of vascular diseases. The unit is also part of the Vascular Research Group and works closely with other research groups at local, national and international levels. The role/Te mahi In this role you will join a multidisciplinary focused clinical research team investigating vascular disease. This position involves working within a fun team of vascular technicians and sonographers in assessing patients with vascular disease, using physiological techniques and ultrasound. Assessments are carried out for the purpose of both clinical patient care and research. The successful applicant would be supported through a two-year post-graduate training programme to learn the specific techniques alongside assisting in the general tasks of the Assessment Unit. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Depending on the qualification of the successful candidate, the intention of the role is to study towards a Vascular Ultrasound qualification and/or a research qualification. You will also have: •    Good organisational skills. •    A professional approach and ability to relate well when dealing with the public. •    High standards of both oral and written communication skills. Further details/Pūroko This is a fixed term, full time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Dunedin, commencing in October 2026 to facilitate introduction to the role and the training pathway. The duration of the training programme is 2 years and commences in February/ March 2027. The position is structured to include 2 years post-qualification if applicable. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The appointment range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640 per annum. If part-time, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information please contact Brigid G. Hill via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference 2600844 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Ms Brigid (Gerry) Hill Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601239": {
      "title": "Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology (2601239) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology (2601239) Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology - 2601239 __________ DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGY | TE TARI WHAKAMĀTAU HINEKARO DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA About our team | Mō te tīma The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has been a renowned place of learning and discovery for more than 150 years. We strive to become an even more successful, globally influential university, committed to upholding the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi as we advance outstanding research and research-informed teaching to enable transformative learning and student experiences. Our Vision / Ko te Aronga is for our people and knowledge to transform lives, connect communities and nurture the environment in Aotearoa, the Pacific, and the world. The Department of Psychology has an excellent research culture and is one of the top Psychology programmes in the world, since the inception of the QS world rankings. We boast excellent computing and technical facilities which support the department’s large and varied teaching and research programmes. Our department provides two professional psychology postgraduate programmes.  Our Postgraduate Diploma in Clinical Psychology (PgDipClPs) is a pathway for students to be eligible to register with the New Zealand Psychologists’ Board in the Clinical Psychology scope of practice.  Our Postgraduate Diploma in Psychological Assessment and Intervention (PgDipPAI) is a pathway for students to be eligible to register in the Psychology Assistant scope of practice. The role | Te mahi We invite applications from suitably qualified and enthusiastic candidates for Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow positions within the Department of Psychology. The successful candidates will contribute to teaching, training, supervision, and programme development across the Department’s professional psychology programmes. Key responsibilities include: •    Delivering teaching and assessment across the PgDipClPs and PgDipPAI programmes. •    Providing clinical supervision to psychology students in the Psychology Clinic and other practice settings. •    Contributing to course coordination, programme administration, and selection and examination panels. •    Integrating culturally responsive and research-informed practice into teaching and clinical training. •    Developing clinical learning opportunities and provide clinic cover as required. This is a unique opportunity for an energetic, creative, and committed individual to make a significant impression and contribution in their chosen discipline while being well supported by a collegial and dynamic Division and University. The successful candidate will be appointed as either a Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow, dependent on skills and experience. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako To be successful in this role candidates must be a New Zealand-registered Psychologist, preferably within the Clinical Psychology or Neuropsychology scope of practice. Candidates will have at least five years’ relevant clinical experience, preferably with most of this experience in mental health settings. Candidates are also expected to have: •    Appropriate professional experience. •    Excellence in case conceptualisation, formulation, and diagnosis. •    A commitment to the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and to equal opportunity. •    Established relationships with the local clinical community is desirable. •    Evidence of an ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels of the institution. Preference may be given to candidates who can provide appropriate cultural supervision and support to staff and students, as well as those with demonstrated ability in teaching and training in areas of current programme needs. We are particularly keen to hear from candidates who can support the programme in developing students’ competence to work in a culturally safe manner with Māori and Pasifika clients. Further details | Pūroko We have up to 2.0 FTE positions available, with options for both permanent and fixed term appointments, at full-time or part-time hours. Hours of work will be negotiated with the successful candidates. Please indicate your preferred employment type and FTE in your cover letter. These positions are available from October 2026 based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children's Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. Specific enquiries about the position may be directed to Associate Professor Deirdre Brown (Clinical Programme Director) and Professor Richard Linscott via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601239 will close on Thursday, 17 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Deirdre Brown Tel: +64 3 556 6416 Contact: Professor Richard Linscott Tel: +64 3 479 5689 Position details: Professional Practice Fellow & Senior Professional Practice Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) or 3 ($105,665 to $114,943) or 4 ($120,495 to $137,444) Organisation Psychology Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601285": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Marketing (2601285) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Marketing (2601285) Teaching Fellow - Marketing - 2601285 __________ DEPARTMENT OF MARKETING | TE MĀTAURANGA TOKOKA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked among the top 1% globally in the QS World University Rankings. The Otago Business School is AACSB- and EQUIS-accredited and recognised as one of Australasia’s leading business schools, offering a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, PhD, and DBA programmes that combine research excellence with a commitment to developing graduates who can think critically and make meaningful contributions. The Department of Marketing within the Otago Business School at the University of Otago is a dynamic and collegial academic community recognised nationally and internationally for excellence in teaching, research, and engagement. The Department offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes that emphasise critical thinking, creativity, and real-world application. Staff are research-active scholars whose work contributes to advancing marketing theory and practice, with strengths in sustainability, consumer behaviour, services marketing, and social marketing. The Department fosters a supportive and inclusive culture that values innovation in teaching, interdisciplinary collaboration, and engagement with industry and community partners. The role | Te mahi The Department of Marketing is seeking to appoint a permanent, 0.8 FTE Teaching Fellow to contribute to the delivery and development of undergraduate marketing teaching. This is an academic year only position, with teaching responsibilities from February to October each year. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching across a range of marketing courses, including MART 112, MART 201, MART 207, MART 211, MART 212, and MART 308. The role will contribute to high-quality teaching, course coordination and/or delivery, assessment, student engagement, and the ongoing enhancement of the Department’s Marketing curriculum. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with colleagues to provide engaging and effective learning experiences and support students throughout their studies. This role will suit an enthusiastic and student-focused educator with expertise across core areas of marketing and a commitment to innovative, inclusive, and evidence-based teaching and learning. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will have: •    A degree in Marketing and demonstrated knowledge across core areas of marketing. •    Demonstrated tertiary teaching experience in Marketing, including undergraduate teaching. •    Demonstrated understanding of tertiary pedagogy and research-informed teaching practice. •    Experience in course delivery and/or coordination, including assessment and curriculum development or enhancement. •    Demonstrated experience in designing and assessing student learning, including providing constructive feedback. •    Demonstrated ability to engage and support students from diverse backgrounds and contribute to an inclusive learning environment. •    Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively. •    A commitment to upholding the values, tikanga, kawa, and traditions of Ngāi Tahu as mana whenua, and to working respectfully within the cultural context of the region. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, part-time (0.8 FTE), academic year only position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin. The academic year is from February to October each year, the exact start and end dates will be confirmed each year. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Dunedin is a vibrant university city with a strong student culture, excellent facilities, and a unique natural environment. The city offers a great lifestyle, with easy access to beaches, outdoor recreation, arts, music, restaurants, and cafés. The University of Otago is committed to Te Tiriti as set out in Pae Tata and Vision 2040. We welcome applications from candidates who bring diverse perspectives and experiences and are committed to providing an inclusive and supportive environment for all staff. Confidential enquiries about the role may be directed to Professor Lisa McNeill, Head of Department, Marketing, via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV •    A cover letter •    3 - 4 Referee contact details, including telephone number and email address To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601285 will close on Thursday, 10 September 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Professor Lisa McNeill Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) Organisation Marketing Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
