    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601012": {
      "title": "Teaching Academic",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) (2601012) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) (2601012) Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) - 2601012 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE / TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO, WELLINGTON / TE WHARE WĀNANGA O ŌTAKOU KI PŌNEKE About our team | Mō te tīma The University of Otago, Wellington (UOW) provides health research and teaching in New Zealand’s capital city. It enjoys collaborative relationships with District Health Boards, local health service providers, multiple central and local government agencies such as the Ministry of Health and Wellington City Council as well as other tertiary education providers such as Massey and Victoria University. It also has strong links with local iwi. Te Tari Hāparapara, Te Tari Whāomoomo / the Department of Surgery and Critical Care at the School of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Otago, Wellington is a multidisciplinary group dedicated to linking biomedical science with clinical research and providing high-quality clinical teaching. The department has research interests spanning cancer, cardiovascular disease, emergency medicine, clinical service provision, and medical education. Teaching in the department includes Surgery and Critical Care modules in the MB ChB programme, and vocationally focused postgraduate qualifications. The role | Te mahi These Lecturer/Senior Lecturer OR Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow roles within our Department represents an exciting opportunity to make a significant contribution to on-going teaching of postgraduate clinical physiology students and medical students. In this role you will be expected to convene and teach within the Postgraduate Certificate and Diploma courses in Medical Technology. The courses are distance taught and aim to provide the academic framework required for those working as a cardiac or sleep physiologist. These courses provide grounding in underlying physiology and pathophysiology for cardiovascular and sleep medicine. In addition, a focus of the programme is on physiological measurement of cardiovascular, respiratory and sleep parameters. The key responsibility for this role will include coordinating and teaching MTEC701 and 702 which are the papers required for the Postgraduate Certificate in Medical Technology. There will be opportunity to contribute to teaching of medical students within the Department of Surgery and Critical Care. The successful candidate for the Lecturer/Senior Lecturer role will be expected to develop an independent research programme, supervise postgraduate research students, and contribute to departmental and school service activities. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will have: •    A qualification in medicine or physiology and relevant clinical experience OR a research qualification (PhD or equivalent) in a field relevant to clinical physiology. •    Expertise in either cardiovascular or sleep medicine fields, or general physiology. •    Experience in teaching within a healthcare or academic environment. •    Commitment to collegiality, teamwork, collaboration and cooperation (both internally and externally) in teaching, supervision and research. •    Excellent oral and written communication skills. You will be expected to commit to developing an understanding of te Tiriti o Waitangi, particularly in relation to health outcomes and the University Māori Strategic Framework. Candidates for the Lecturer/Senior Lecturer role, will have advanced academic qualifications and currently research active in a related field. Further details | Pūroko We have permanent positions at both full-time and part-time hours available (up to 1.3 FTE) from 1 October 2026, based in Wellington. Hours of work are negotiable, and candidates are asked to indicate their preference in their cover letter. Appointments will be made at Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Professional Practice Fellow, or Senior Professional Practice Fellow level, depending on the successful candidate's skills, experience and academic achievements. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for these positions. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information about this role please contact Associate Professor Kirsty Danielson, Head of Department via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including cover letter and CV), please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601012 will close on Wednesday, 16 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received and the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: If this is a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range. Contact: Associate Professor Kirsty Danielson Position details: Lecturer/Senior Lecturer , Professional Practice Fellow and Senior Professional Practice Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601129": {
      "title": "Working",
      "description": "Working at Otago - Your Career, University of Otago (s01) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Back to prior page This page is displayed in error. Please visit the main vacancies page for all current vacancies. ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601152": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East (2601152) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East (2601152) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East - 2601152 __________ THE POLITICS PROGRAMME | TŌRAKAPŪ DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI About us | Mō mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked in the top 1% in the QS World University Rankings. Our Politics Programme was established in 1967 and has a well-established record of teaching and research. We offer a range of research-informed undergraduate and postgraduate courses in comparative politics/area studies, Aotearoa New Zealand politics, international relations, and political theory. We have a strong record of working in both teaching and research with scholars and programmes in the Division of Humanities and from across the university. The Politics Programme has a long-standing commitment to teaching and researching Politics of the Middle East, having for over twenty-five years offered a range of courses at undergraduate and postgraduate levels and supervised a significant number of research students in this area. The Programme was instrumental in establishing MEISA (Middle East and Islamic Studies Aotearoa), an interdisciplinary academic network that promotes research on the Middle East and Islamic worlds from a New Zealand perspective. The role | Te mahi Applications are invited for a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer position (Politics of the Middle East) in the Politics Programme. The position offers an academic career in a well-established programme with a proud teaching and research record. We are looking for a candidate with a demonstrated record of producing engaging, innovative teaching and research. Our ideal candidate will contribute to existing courses and develop new courses in Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics. The successful candidate will teach lower-level undergraduate courses on Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics, develop a 3rd year course in their area of research expertise, and either contribute to existing courses at Master’s level or develop their own course in their area of research expertise. They will be expected to: •    supervise research at Master’s and PhD levels; •    develop and maintain a strong research profile, including showing a willingness to seek external research funds; and •    undertake administrative roles and community/professional service activities that contribute to the overall effectiveness and standing of the Programme. Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeke me kā wheako The successful candidate is expected to have a PhD in Politics or related discipline and to be able to demonstrate a record of high-quality research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate will also demonstrate: •    A commitment to being a productive collaborator with a track record of working collegially. •    An excellent research profile along with a commitment to and/or established connections with international and/or national researchers. •    A record of delivering high-quality tertiary-level teaching. •    Ability in teaching Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics. •    A working knowledge of at least one Middle Eastern language. •    A commitment to deliver their own specialist courses to our programme. •    A commitment to contribute to team-taught courses. •    A commitment to undertake media and community outreach and engagement. •    Experience working with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds. Early career staff have a managed workload programme to enable them to develop their research activities through the first two years or so of their appointment. There will also be opportunities to complete a number of professional development courses related to the Treaty of Waitangi, te reo Māori, te aō Māori, and engaging with the University’s Māori and Pacific Strategic Frameworks. Further Information | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (1 FTE) position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, and is available from February 2027. The appointment ranges for this role are: •    Lecturer - $97,377 to $ 114,943 per annum. •    Senior Lecturer - $ 120,494 to $137,444 per annum. Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Associate Professor James Headley, Head of Politics Programme, via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV, including employment history. •    A letter of application outlining how you fit the role. •    Names and addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601152 will close on 11:59pm (NZST) on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor James Headley Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) or Senior Lecturer / Senior Research Fellow ($120,494 to $137,444) Organisation Politics Job Function Research and Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601160": {
      "title": "Working",
      "description": "Working at Otago - Your Career, University of Otago (s01) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Back to prior page This page is displayed in error. Please visit the main vacancies page for all current vacancies. ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601192": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry (2601192) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry (2601192) Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry - 2601192 __________ DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA DEPARTMENT OF CHEMISTRY | TE TARI MĀTAURANGA MATŪ Mō te tīma | About our team The University of Otago, established in 1869, has a long and distinguished tradition of teaching and research. The Department of Chemistry at the University of Otago is the oldest chemistry department in New Zealand. It has grown to be one of the largest Departments in the Division of Sciences and contributes to various courses and programmes in Sciences and Health Sciences. Responsibility for the Department's dual mandate of teaching and research is shared among full-time academic staff and a number of teaching and research fellows, assisted by a similar number of technical, secretarial and general staff. As such, the Department is one of the largest and most active in New Zealand, with working conditions and facilities that rival those of many overseas departments. The role | Te mahi The Royal Society of New Zealand Marsden Fund has awarded the Ennis group a research grant to investigate the formation and structure of mixed clathrate hydrate minerals relevant to Titan’s surface; including the study of possible astrochemical pathways toward higher-order organic molecules. This postdoctoral fellow position is to support and advance all objectives of this laboratory astrochemistry research project, including the coordination of overseas experiments with expert collaborators at ANSTO Melbourne and Sydney, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory California USA and the University of Western Australia. Key tasks of the role include: •    Contributing to the design and development of new laboratory capabilities including gas delivery manifolds, thin film deposition and thickness verification, vacuum hardware, photolysis setups. •    Carrying out the optimisation and maintenance of sample synthesis equipment, high vacuum equipment, gas handling systems and cryocooler hardware. •    Synthesis, purification and handling hazardous gaseous samples including astronomical nitriles. •    Performing data analysis including vibrational spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, X-ray and neutron diffraction crystallography. Perform supporting computations such as DFT calculations. •    Contributing toward novel experiment design. •    Liaising with international team to coordinate remote experiments and write beamline proposals. •    Assisting with the writing of scientific papers and journal articles. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Your skills and experience Successful candidates will have: •    A PhD in a relevant field (such as chemistry, physics, planetary science, or materials science). •    Previous experience with synthesis and purification of gas samples, including knowledge of safe handling and disposal procedures. •    Previous experience with laboratory high vacuum and cryogenic systems as applied to astrochemistry or surface science studies. •    Previous experience with powder x-ray and/or neutron diffraction and associated data analysis routines not essential, but advantageous. Pūroko | Further details This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), fixed-term position (for 2 years), available from 1 March 2027, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The salary for this position is $92,350 per annum. Applications from candidates with Māori and Pacific backgrounds or strong competencies in these areas are warmly encouraged. The Dunedin campus is consistently ranked among the most beautiful in the world. The city offers affordable living, a safe environment, and everything within walking distance. Spectacular outdoor activities await right at the doorstep. For further information, please contact Dr Courtney Ennis, via the contact details below. Tono | Application Candidates are requested to submit: •    Cover letter •    Curriculum Vitae, including a list of publications •    Names and contact details of three referees To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601192 will close at 11:59 PM (NZST) on Sunday, 20 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Dr Courtney Ennis Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Postdoctoral Fellow ($92,350) Organisation Chemistry Job Function Research ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601163": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) (2601163) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) (2601163) Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) - 2601163 __________ FACULTY OF MEDICINE | TE KAUPEKA WHAIORA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO WELLINGTON | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA, PŌNEKE About our team | Mō te tīma We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to join the Faculty of Medicine at University of Otago, Wellington and also under a separate contract support the Clinical Trials Unit (CTU) at Te Whatu Ora Capital and Coast and Hutt Valley. The role | Te mahi This is an exciting opportunity for a resident medical officer (with MCNZ General Registration at time of appointment) to gain broad exposure to academic medicine through a unique shared appointment between Te Whatu Ora – Capital, Coast and Hutt Valley and the Faculty of Medicine, University of Otago, Wellington. The role combines clinical trial support and medical student teaching. Key responsibilities include: •    Support ongoing clinical trials, including protocol familiarisation, data collection, patient engagement, and trial coordination tasks. •    Contribute to ethics applications, data management, and publication drafts. •    Assist in delivery of clinical teaching in MBChB modules and contribute to assessments and OSCEs. •    Participate in CTU meetings and assist with audit and quality assurance activities. •    Build collaborative relationships with clinician-researchers and partner institutions. Your Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    Registered medical practitioner in New Zealand (with MCNZ General Registration at time of appointment). •    Demonstrated interest in clinical research and medical education. •    Strong organisational and communication skills. •    Ability to work independently and as part of a team. •    Self-directed and motivated individual, proactive in identifying and pursuing opportunities •    Understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and commitment to its principles. •    Desirable: prior experience in clinical trials or teaching. Further details | Pūroko This is a combined fixed-term, full-time (1.0 FTE) position for 12 months, located in Pōneke, Wellington.  A 0.8 FTE position is negotiable for the right candidate. The position will be based in Wellington Regional Hospital, Hutt Hospital, and University of Otago, Wellington campus. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The salary range for the academic position is $98,583 to $120,528 per annum (pro rata) - the level of appointment will be based on skills and experience. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For more information regarding the role, please contact Professor Rebecca Grainger via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601163 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received and suitable candidates may be interviewed. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Professor Rebecca Grainger Position details: Teaching Fellow & Research Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow - Medical & Dental ($98,583 to $120,528) Organisation Medicine Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600967": {
      "title": "Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland)",
      "description": "Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) - 2600967 __________ CLINICAL SERVICES FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Are you a new or recent dental graduate seeking an exciting opportunity to join a supportive culture founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility? We are looking for passionate and driven graduate dentists to join our modern team based dental practice at our facilities in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The role | Te mahi The Faculty of Dentistry invites applications for up to four full-time fixed term positions across both campuses with the possibility of three positions in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and one position in Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland, commencing 2027. In these roles you will work with experienced clinicians to provide a wide scope of general dental procedures including urgent care. Throughout this placement, you can benefit from ongoing mentoring, training, support, and coaching. These early career positions offer a unique opportunity where you can have the ability to gain valuable clinical experience under the mentorship of experienced dentists and academics within the Faculty of Dentistry, University of Otago. You can build and enhance your clinical skills in a culturally supportive environment that includes dental treatment delivering planned treatments and urgent care. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We seek Dentists within their first three years of working, who could value interacting with other professionals and who thrive on working in a supportive environment founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility. You aspire to form a firm foundation of clinical skills and there can be opportunities to get involved in teaching, research, or postgraduate learning. To be successful in this role, you should also have: •    Strong knowledge and understanding in all areas of general dentistry. •    Exceptional communication and patient care skills. •    An ability to work in a large cohesive team environment. •    Outstanding time management and organisational skills. •    A strong desire to consolidate your clinical skills in a supportive environment. •    An ability to provide high quality dental care to all age groups of patients. •    A drive to support students as a mentor in areas commensurate with your clinical expertise. •    Knowledge or the ability to acquire knowledge of procedures, policies, regulations and standards of practice. Applicants must be registered or registrable with the Dental Council of New Zealand (DCNZ). Further details | Puroko We have multiple, full-time (1 FTE), fixed term (18 January 2027 – 14 January 2028) positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin or Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The salary for these positions is $79,812 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. For further information, please contact Yolin Govender (Auckland) or Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2600967 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Yolin Govender (Auckland) Contact: Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Clinical Services Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2501589": {
      "title": "Senior Professional Practice Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry - 2501589 __________ DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities. Founded in 1907, the Faculty of Dentistry is based at the University’s main campus in Dunedin with clinics in Auckland and South Dunedin and is the only dental school in New Zealand. We are proud of our international reputation for research, clinical, and academic excellence. The 2023 QS World University Subject Rankings list the Dental School as 42nd in the world. The Faculty of Dentistry comprises of three Departments (Oral Rehabilitation, Oral Diagnostic and Surgical and Oral Sciences) as well as the Sir John Walsh Research Institute. Dunedin offers an outstanding quality of life, combining the vibrancy of a university city with the warmth of a close-knit community. The region provides exceptional schooling and educational opportunities, making it an ideal place for families. Dunedin is perfectly placed for outdoor pursuits, with world-class mountain bike trails, nearby ski fields, and excellent surfing. Just a few hours from Queenstown—the adventure capital of New Zealand—it also offers easy access to some of the country’s most stunning natural landscapes. Dunedin’s wildlife is world-renowned, home to the royal albatross, seals, penguins, and an array of native birds. ‘The outdoors and wildlife was one of our biggest pulls to this area as a family coming from the UK - also the schools are fantastic!’  H. Smith, Senior Professional Practice Fellow. The role | Te mahi The University is seeking applications from suitably qualified candidates for the position of Senior Professional Practice Fellows in Dentistry. As a Senior Professional Practice Fellow, you will help support teaching, facilitate learning, assist with assessments, and carry out clinical service/clinical activity both on undergraduate clinics and staff clinics out of term time. Some key tasks of the role include: •    Clinical teaching/tutoring of undergraduate dentists. •    Contributing to the Department’s academic goals by applying expert clinical knowledge in your area of specialty. •    Preparation, assessment, teaching and paper administration duties. •    Following through patient care and referrals within the hospital. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Successful applicants: •    Must hold a dental degree and have registration or be eligible for full registration with the Dental Council of New Zealand. •    Will demonstrate the ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels within the institution. •    Show a commitment to uphold the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and the University’s Māori Strategic Framework. Candidates will also have and maintain registration (if supervised) including: •    A practicing certificate in place before starting work. •    Indemnity in place prior to starting work. Further details | Pūroko We have eight job openings, offering both permanent and fixed-term positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. We have opportunities for Dentists with special interests in Endodontics, Prosthodontics and Periodontics. Both full-time (1 FTE) and part-time positions are available, allowing for employment over the full year or semester time only. The duration of any fixed-term positions can be negotiable with the successful candidates. International candidates would need to be available to work minimum 0.8FTE. The salary range for these positions is $109,554 to $184,993 per annum (pro rata). Candidates will be appointed to a step on the Senior Professional Practice Fellow scale 3 or 4, dependent on qualifications and skills. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children’s Act 2014.  Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. Specific enquiries about this position may be directed to Dr Pip MacDonald via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications should quote reference number 2501598. There is no formal closing date for this role. We may begin interviewing suitable candidates at any  time during the recruitment process and reserve the right to make an appointment before the advertised closing date. LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Dr Pip MacDonald Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website & Faculty of Dentistry Video Tour Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow - Medical & Dental 3 ($109,554 to $120,528) or 4 ($148,414 to $184,993) Organisation Office of the Head of Faculty (Dentistry) Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601399": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Business Analyst  (2601399) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Business Analyst  (2601399) Senior Business Analyst - 2601399 __________ IT INFORMATION SYSTEMS DIGITAL DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO About us | Mō mātou Digital Services provides the technology, systems, and support that enable teaching, learning, research, and operations across the University. As one of New Zealand's largest ICT environments, we're delivering an exciting programme of work to strengthen our data platform capability and develop new learner insights products that support better decision-making across the University. The role | Te mahi This is an opportunity to play a key role in delivering new data and insights capabilities that will help improve outcomes for learners. Working closely with stakeholders, data specialists, architects, and engineers, you'll identify business needs, define requirements, and help shape products that turn data into meaningful insights. In this role you will: •    Lead business analysis activities across data platform and learner insights initiatives. •    Translate business needs into clear requirements and priorities that support delivery. •    Work with stakeholders and technical teams to define data requirements, metrics, and outcomes. •    Help shape data products that improve decision-making and learner outcomes. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You are an experienced Senior Business Analyst who thrives in complex environments and enjoys working at the intersection of business, data, and technology. With strong analytical and stakeholder engagement skills, you're able to bring clarity to ambiguity, build trusted relationships, and translate complex ideas into practical solutions. Essential: •    Proven senior-level business analysis experience delivering complex digital, data, or technology initiatives. •    Strong stakeholder engagement and facilitation skills, with the ability to work effectively across business and technical teams. •    Experience defining business requirements, processes, and outcomes that support successful delivery. •    Strong understanding of data and analytics, including translating business needs into meaningful insights. •    Relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent professional experience. Preferred: •    Experience working with data platforms, analytics, business intelligence, or reporting environments. •    Experience within education, learner insights, or customer-focused data environments. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), 18-month fixed-term position. While Dunedin is our preferred location, flexible and remote working arrangements will be considered. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information regarding the role please contact Luke Erickson, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601399 will close on Sunday, 20 September. Applications may be reviewed on receipt Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Luke Erickson Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Digital Projects Job Function Project Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601396": {
      "title": "Fleet and Parking Manager",
      "description": "Job Description - Fleet and Parking Manager (2601396) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Fleet and Parking Manager (2601396) Fleet and Parking Manager - 2601396 __________ PROPERTY AND CAMPUS DEVELOPMENT | KĀ RAWA ME TE WHANAKEHAKA PAPA TAURIMA About our team | Mō te tīma The Property and Campus Development team supports the ongoing academic mission of Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka by providing the best facilities, services and physical environment in which staff, students and the public can learn, teach, live and play. Everyone benefits directly and indirectly from the dedicated efforts of Property Services Division employees. We're the physical foundation of the teaching, research and learning that takes place at the University, and we're proud of the important role we play. The Division is responsible for buildings and more than 22,000 hectares of land. With over 3,500 University Staff and over 20,000 students. The role | Te mahi The Fleet and Parking Manager plays a vital role in overseeing the University’s vehicle fleet and parking operations, ensuring that all vehicles—from electric carts to vans and specialist equipment—are kept safe, reliable, and ready for use. This hands-on role involves managing daily fleet activities, including scheduling maintenance, coordinating repairs, and conducting inspections to meet strict safety and compliance standards. The Manager leads the strategic procurement and replacement of fleet assets, balancing cost efficiency with the University’s growing commitment to sustainability through the adoption of low-emission and electric vehicles. The Manager works with the Parking Coordinator to optimise space use, ensure compliance, and meet campus needs. The role also involves risk assessments, audits, health and safety oversight, and occasional first aid or emergency support, playing a key part in the University’s operations. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You bring a tertiary qualification in Logistics, Business Administration, Facilities Management, certification in Fleet Management, or equivalent professional experience. Your proven track record in fleet management or transport logistics showcases your deep expertise in vehicle maintenance, procurement, and compliance. With exceptional organisational and time-management skills, coupled with strong budgeting and cost-control capabilities, you meet all necessary licensing requirements to operate fleet vehicles confidently. You thrive on building meaningful, collaborative partnerships and take pride in connecting with diverse stakeholders to drive success together. Other key skills include: •    Knowledge of electric vehicle technology and infrastructure, understanding of sustainability in transportation. •    Proficient in Microsoft Excel and fleet or asset management software for tracking and reporting. •    Ability to analyse and report on vehicle usage, maintenance schedules, and operational costs. •    Preferred experience with vehicle telematics, GPS tracking systems, fuel efficiency tools, and sustainability initiatives. •    Familiarity with fleet management software and parking enforcement is highly desirable. •    Flexibility to work outside standard hours when necessary. •    Dedication to working in a culturally safe and respectful manner and demonstrates an understanding and appreciation of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles, and Pasifika values. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $75,631 to $80,503. Top of Range $88,750 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in Aotearoa, New Zealand to be considered for this position. Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka | University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. We welcome the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives. For further information, please contact Melissa Symister via the details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601396 will close on Wednesday, 30 September. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Melissa Symister Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Quality, Performance and Ancillary Services Job Function Administrative and Operations Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601394": {
      "title": "Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations)",
      "description": "Job Description - Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations) (2601394) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations) (2601394) Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations) - 2601394 __________ VICE-CHANCELLOR'S OFFICE | TE TARI O TE TUMU WHAKARAE Help shape the future of one of New Zealand's most influential institutions The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is Aotearoa New Zealand's first and most research-intensive university, with a strong commitment to excellence, Te Tiriti o Waitangi and a bold vision for the future. We are seeking an outstanding senior leader to take on the role of Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations). Reporting directly to the Vice-Chancellor, this newly established role will provide strategic leadership across the University's operational and service functions, ensuring the systems, people, infrastructure and services that underpin our academic mission are integrated, future focused and positioned for long term success. Te mahi | The role You will be one of the University's most senior leaders, responsible for ensuring the operational capability, organisational performance and strategic infrastructure required to deliver Otago's academic mission. Leading a diverse portfolio that spans People and Culture; Digital Technologies; Property and Campus Development; Student Services and Pastoral Care; Operations; Sustainability; Risk, Assurance and Compliance; and Health, Safety and Wellbeing, you will bring together services, systems and people to create an integrated, future focused operating environment. This is a role of significant scale and influence. You will partner closely with other university leaders including the Vice-Chancellor, Provost and fellow Deputy Vice-Chancellors to drive strategic priorities, lead institutional transformation and strengthen the foundations that support teaching, research and student success. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Your skills and experience You will be an accomplished executive with experience leading the operations of large, complex organisations through periods of change and transformation. Equally adept at shaping strategy and delivering results, you will be recognised for your ability to build organisational capability, inspire outstanding leaders, and align people, services and resources behind a shared vision. You will bring strong commercial and governance expertise, exceptional stakeholder management skills, and the credibility to influence at the highest levels.  You will have knowledge, and ideally experience, of the work of universities and a commitment to supporting student and staff success. Above all, you will be motivated by the opportunity to help shape the future of one of New Zealand's most respected institutions and contribute to outcomes that make a meaningful difference for our students, staff and communities. Pūroko | Further details This is a full time (1.0 FTE), permanent position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University of Otago values diverse perspectives and is committed to creating an inclusive and respectful workplace where staff and students can thrive. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact Elena Calvert, Recruitment Manager, via the contact details below. Tono | Application To apply, please submit your CV and a cover letter via the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601394 close on Wednesday, 30 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Elena Calvert Position details: Job Description & Information for Candidates Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Vice-Chancellor's Office Job Function Academic Leadership ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601382": {
      "title": "Hocken Collections Assistant",
      "description": "Job Description - Hocken Collections Assistant (2601382) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Hocken Collections Assistant (2601382) Hocken Collections Assistant - 2601382 __________ ACADEMIC DIVISION | TE WĀHAKA MATUA MĀTAURAKA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO LIBRARY | TE PĀTAKA MĀTAURANGA O ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA THE HOCKEN LIBRARY | TE WHATA IRI KURA O HĀKENA Are you passionate about heritage materials, possess an eye for detail and a commitment to collection care, appreciate cross-organizational workflows, and have a flair for sharing collections knowledge with diverse research communities? The role | Te mahi The University Library is seeking a Collections Assistant (Hocken Published Collections) to join our team. In this collections and research focused role, you’ll work in a team to provide meticulous collection management, while also ensuring that researchers and communities can discover and access Hocken collections. You’ll work closely with Collection Assistants, Archivists, Curators, and Librarians in the Hocken and University Libraries to meticulously maintain Hocken’s significant published collections. You’ll also spend time in person assisting and interacting with students, staff and communities to positively guide their use of Hocken collections to fulfil their diverse research needs. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako If you appreciate the heritage of Aotearoa New Zealand and the Pacific, while also being a systems thinker with an eye for detail interested in providing access to collections, this role will offer you the opportunity to work with nationally significant heritage collections, contributing to the research success of the Hocken’s diverse stakeholders. What you'll bring: •    Sound general knowledge of New Zealand and Pacific history and culture. •    Experience working in a library, archive, museum or related heritage institution. •    A creative, problem-solving mindset and enthusiasm for learning. •    Excellent communication and customer service skills. •    A commitment to precision and the ability to be accurate at all times. •    Competency in the use of computers, including familiarity with databases and Microscft Office and an ability to adapt to new systems. •    The capacity to work as a member of a highly competent team without direct supervision. About our team | Mō te tīma The Hocken is one of the foremost research libraries in New Zealand celebrating the histories, culture and natural environment of Aotearoa New Zealand and the Pacific. The Hocken is part of the University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka Library, which provides high-quality information resources and services, recognising the diversity and needs of all stakeholders. Our expert teams strive to be responsive and innovative in our service delivery and to be a vital contributor to the University and wider community. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position, Monday to Friday, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The position will be required to work occasional rostered half-days (3.5 hours) on Saturdays. The appointment range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185.Top of range $73,640. Please note that some physical work is involved, including handling collection material and working at height. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence - please contact Kirstie Ross, Head Curator Published and Special Collections via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601382 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Kirstie Ross Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Hocken Library Job Function Library and Information Services ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601349": {
      "title": "Mental Health Support Clinician",
      "description": "Job Description - Mental Health Support Clinician (2601349) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Mental Health Support Clinician (2601349) Mental Health Support Clinician - 2601349 __________ STUDENT HEALTH SERVICES | TE RATOKA HAUORA ĀKOKA About our team | Mō te tīma Student Health Services (SHS) provide a full range of primary health care services for students at the University of Otago. We have an experienced team of GPs, Nurses, Mental Health and Wellbeing clinicians, and Administration staff who provide approximately 45,000 appointments per annum. The large and diverse health team and the distinctive campus provide a stimulating and challenging multidisciplinary work environment. The University of Otago’s aspires to be a Te Tiriti-led university that is committed to partnership with mana whenua and upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Student Health Services is committed to equity and diversity and seeks to provide an accessible, respectful and welcoming environment in which all students and staff receive the individual support and resources they need. SHS promotes and upholds equity in its processes and values the individual differences that enrich its community. We are recognised as a Rainbow Tick certified employer and a Foundation certified and Cornerstone accredited practice, which means we meet a standard of inclusivity and manaakitanga for our staff and students, and we are committed to continuous improvement. The role | Te mahi This Mental Health Support role has a strong focus on assessment and triage. Primarily you will be conducting one off assessments, then developing collaborative plans of support with students who are presenting to the service. Robust and enhanced risk assessment skills are required. Experience of working in similar roles is strongly desirable. You will work in conjunction with a multi-disciplinary team of mental health professionals and the wider primary care team at Student Health Services. A stepped care approach is required as well as a sound working knowledge of available referral partners and community resources. In this role, you will report to the Clinical Group Leader of The Mental Health and Wellbeing Team. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Ideally you will be an experienced and autonomous mental health clinician who communicates clearly and works well in various team environments. A proven ability to function well under pressure of high clinical demand is essential. Successful candidate will also: •    Be suitably qualified within the mental health field with a strong clinical background in mental health assessment and triage. •    Have current registration with a professional body and APC. •    Have a strong interest and experience in helping young adults. •    Demonstrate experience with assessment, short-term intervention and liaison work (essential). Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. Hours of work are between 8:30am – 8:30pm on a rostered basis, as negotiated. The salary range for this role is $89,180 to $113,818 per annum. The successful candidate will be appointed on an appropriate step of the scale dependent on qualifications, skills, and experience. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence please contact Richard Mooney, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601349 will close on Thursday, 01 October 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be asked to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Richard Mooney Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Wellbeing Counsellor / Mental Health Support ($89,180 to $113,818) Organisation Student Health Services Job Function Health Professional ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601371": {
      "title": "Deputy Warden",
      "description": "Job Description - Deputy Warden | Tautiaki-Piki - University College (2601371) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Deputy Warden | Tautiaki-Piki - University College (2601371) Deputy Warden | Tautiaki-Piki - University College - 2601371 __________ UNIVERSITY COLLEGE CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES Otago is famous for its residential college communities supporting thousands of mostly first-year students as they begin their tertiary journey at the University of Otago. The Tautiaki-Piki of Te Kāreti o te Whare Wānaka plays a central role in sharing manaakitanga to everyone. Our main aim is to foster a strong sense of whanaungatanga as an essential foundation for learning and personal growth of Unicol tauira. The Role / Te Mahi The Tautiaki-Piki (Deputy Warden) is responsible to the Tautiaki (College Warden).  As a key senior member of staff, the Tautiaki-Piki has a leadership role in providing a positive collegiate experience for residents, with a focus on resident oraka (wellbeing). We are seeking a great communicator to join our team who can support the academic ambitions and pastoral needs of our residents. Our college provides positive student-centred support, academic programmes and an extensive range of events and activities. A safe place to set down roots, where students feel secure knowing the staff will unconditionally support them on their journey. Skills & Experience / Kā Pūkeka me kā Wheako You will be someone who thrives in a busy role and excels in providing pastoral care and support to students from a variety of backgrounds and cultures. Our ideal candidate will:- •    Be professionally and personally resilient and capable of handling (sometimes) difficult and complex situations. •    Have experience working with young adults in a leadership capacity. •    Be knowledgeable and have experience in pastoral care and/or community development. •    Have well developed organisational skills and initiative. •    Be able to effectively coordinate the college academic and tutorial programme. •    Deputise in the absence of the Tautiaki (College Warden). •    Be trusted to act in a calm manner. An understanding of, and empathy for, students commencing University is fundamental to this role. Experience in the tertiary sector would be a distinct advantage. Candidates with knowledge and appreciation of diverse cultural needs, of Tīkaka Māori, and/or knowledge in providing positive opportunities for both Tauira Māori and Pasifika students will be especially welcome. Further Details / Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time role that will require regular rostered evening and weekend hours of work. The position is live-in and requires a high level of commitment, including being rostered on call. The Deputy Warden residence is a separate 3-bedroom house/apartment situated adjacent to the North Tower with a private entry to the car park and Clyde Street. It has one bathroom, and a small office space/storage space. There is an open plan kitchen and living room and two car parks in the swipe access internal parking area. The total remuneration package for this position includes full compensation for all accommodation costs. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children’s Act 2014 and the Education (Pastoral Care of Tertiary and International Learners) Code of Practice 2021. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a police safety check. Confidential enquiries can be made to Chris Jacobs, Acting Tautiaki/Warden of University College via the contact details below. Application/ Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601371 will close Sunday, 27 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Chris Jacobs Tel: +64 21 278 5990 Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 7 (Range of Appointments $80,749 to $85,649, Top of Range $100,776) Organisation University College Job Function Advice, Support and Welfare ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601384": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "Job Description - Project Manager - Cyber Security & Identity (2601384) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Project Manager - Cyber Security & Identity (2601384) Project Manager - Cyber Security & Identity - 2601384 __________ DIGITAL SERVICES DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO Come join the University and be part of its success as the University’s cyber security Project Manager for our roadmap of projects! About us | Mō mātou Here at Digital Services, we are responsible for the provision, maintenance, and support of information and communication technologies and business systems within the University of Otago community. We are one of the largest ICT operations in NZ, supporting tens of thousands of users, a big network across multiple campuses and a huge range of systems. The role | Te mahi You will be part of our exciting roadmap of cyber security projects, getting the opportunity to lead cyber projects that help our whole University Community. You will manage the end-to-end delivery of challenging, high-value cyber projects, working within the Digital Projects Office, and the wider Digital Services team. You will take ownership of the projects from concept to closure, and will liaise closely with all stakeholders, vendors, and users. In this role you will be: •    Working closely with our CISO, leading multiple projects within our cyber security roadmap. •    Managing projects end to end, working with technical experts, wider digital services, and stakeholders across our whole university community. •    Working in a growing digital projects team with a chance to contribute to how we work. •    No boring run of the mill or 'easy' projects - lots of opportunities and challenges to get your teeth into! Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You are an experienced project manager, having led and loved working in cyber security projects. You need to have a good understanding of digital environments, and engaging with a diverse stakeholder group. You will be a great people person, with a positive proactive attitude and a willingness to get things done. You will be able to manage multi-functional teams, leading them to deliver great business outcomes. A sense of humour would also be useful! As you will be working in an agile manner, it's important that you enjoy problem solving, are an effective communicator and can adapt to shifting priorities. You will be dealing with a range of internal and external stakeholders, so it's important that you can build productive and positive relationships with a range of people. Essential: •    A relevant tertiary level qualification (in project management, information technology or a relevant business discipline) and/or recognised qualification(s) appropriate to the role (e.g. PRINCE2, PMP). •    Considerable experience in Information Technology (IT) project management and delivering IT projects. •    Proven experience working with project management methodologies and tools, including scoping, planning, resourcing and monitoring project outcomes. •    Proven ability to analyse and interpret information, prepare written reports, deal with challenges creatively and achieve business, client focused solutions. •    Demonstrated commercial acumen. Preferred: •    Extensive experience in cyber security project management in a large or complex organisation. •    Demonstrated project leadership experience. •    Demonstrated experience in procurement and contract administration, contract and consultant management. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours), fixed term position for 18 months. We have a strong preference that the role is located in Ōtepoti/Dunedin, however we will consider remote work. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information regarding the role please contact Aram Karim, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601384 will close on Wednesday, 23 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Aram Karim Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Digital Projects Job Function Project Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601321": {
      "title": "Senior Analyst Test and Performance",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Analyst Test and Performance (2601321) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Analyst Test and Performance (2601321) Senior Analyst Test and Performance - 2601321 __________ DIGITAL SERVICES DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO The University of Otago is transforming how we support our people. Our People Pathway (OPP) is a major programme delivering a new Human Resources Information Management (Workday) system across the University. We are seeking a Senior Test Analyst to support delivery of this high-impact programme. The role | Te mahi This role sits within Our People Pathway, the University's Workday implementation programme, supporting the delivery of new people, process, data and technology capability across the University. Working across business and technical teams, you will provide testing expertise, coordinate testing activities, and help ensure solutions are fit for purpose and meet business needs. This is an opportunity to contribute to a significant enterprise transformation that will improve how staff experience core HR services. Work will include: •    Planning and coordinating testing activities across the Workday programme. •    Testing Workday, Dayforce aPay and integrations with other University systems. •    Leading and coordinating User Acceptance Testing (UAT) with business stakeholders and subject matter experts. •    Working with implementation partners and technical teams to improve testing approaches. •    Supporting functional, integration, end-to-end and non-functional testing activities. •    Managing defects, test evidence and reporting to support quality outcomes. •    Supporting test data, test environment and test readiness activities across the implementation lifecycle. •    Working collaboratively with stakeholders, Digital Services and implementation partners to support delivery, change readiness and transition to business-as-usual operations. Skills and Experience | Kā Pūkeka Me Kā Wheako We are looking for a Senior Test Analyst who can bring expertise and practical thinking to complex work, work effectively with a wide range of stakeholders, and support the delivery of high-quality outcomes. To succeed in this role, you will bring: •    Proven experience coordinating testing activities for complex technology projects or transformation initiatives. •    Strong experience in test planning, execution, defect management and reporting. •    Experience analysing requirements and translating them into effective test scenarios and test cases. •    Experience leading and coordinating User Acceptance Testing with business stakeholders and subject matter experts. •    Experience testing complex, integrated systems across multiple platforms, including integration and end-to-end testing. •    Strong analytical, problem-solving and stakeholder engagement skills. •    Excellent communication skills, with the ability to explain testing concepts, risks and outcomes clearly. The following would be an advantage: •    Experience testing ERP, HR or payroll systems. •    Knowledge of Workday or other Human Resources Information Systems. •    Experience with API testing. •    ISTQB or another recognised testing qualification. •    Experience with performance testing or performance measurement tools. •    Knowledge of accessibility or security testing. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), fixed-term position for 2 years, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $99,364 to $107,888. Top of range $114,280. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information or to discuss the roles in confidence please contact Alex Beaton via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601321 will close Wednesday 23, September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Alex Beaton Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Digital Specialist Band D (Range of Appointments $99,364 to $107,888, Top of Range $114,280) Organisation Information Systems Job Function ICT Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601369": {
      "title": "Senior Technician",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Technician - Botany  (2601369) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Technician - Botany  (2601369) Senior Technician - Botany - 2601369 __________ DEPARTMENT OF BOTANY | TE TARI HUAOTA DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA About us | Mō mātou The Department of Botany is the only university department in New Zealand specialising in the science of plants and other functionally related organisms which include seaweeds, cyanobacteria, fungi, lichens, and phytoplankton. Research and teaching involve laboratory through to field settings. The role | Te mahi In the role of Senior Technician, you will be responsible for the resourcing of teaching and research programmes, compliance management, management of the technical team, management of facilities and security issues. The position will involve both laboratory and field-based work. The Department of Botany is active in research, and the position will also entail oversight of support for research programmes. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Our ideal candidate will have relevant graduate qualification with experience of laboratory management or an extensive equivalent combination of education, training, and experience. You will have a knowledge and understanding of the field of botany and experience with New Zealand biosecurity processes. Other skills required: •    Excellent technical knowledge, including knowledge of hazardous substances and their properties, laboratory and field skills. •    Competent with computers and Office software. •    Provision of proactive leadership and direction for the technical team. •    Proven ability to establish and maintain credible and productive relationships with key internal and external stakeholders. •    Proven ability to maintain confidentiality and a high standard of professionalism at all times is essential. •    Commitment to the Department of Botany and its future is essential. •    Results focused; anticipates problems and proactively resolves them in an appropriate manner. •    Excellent analytical skills. •    Strong organisational and project management skills; ability to meet timelines and budgets. •    Demonstrates high attention to detail. •    Ability to effectively and diplomatically relate to a diverse range of people. •    Liaise with other parts of the University as required, to meet customer needs. If you have exceptional technical expertise with an eye for detail and a keenness to provide outstanding support to students and staff, we encourage you to apply for this exciting position. Further details | Pūroko This is a full time, fixed term (12 month) position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $80,749 to $85,649. Top of range $100,776. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information please contact David Orlovich, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601369 will close on Tuesday, 29 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: David Orlovich Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 7 (Range of Appointments $80,749 to $85,649, Top of Range $100,776) Organisation Botany Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601380": {
      "title": "HR Adviser",
      "description": "Job Description - HR Adviser (2601380) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - HR Adviser (2601380) HR Adviser - 2601380 __________ HUMAN RESOURCES DIVISION | TE WĀHANGA MATUA PŪMANAWA TANGATA Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has been a renowned place of learning and discovery for more than 150 years. We strive to become an even more successful, globally influential university, committed to upholding the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi as we advance outstanding research and research-informed teaching to enable transformative learning and student experiences. Our Vision/Ko te Aronga is for our people and knowledge to transform lives, connect communities and nurture the environment in Ōtākou, Te Waipounamu, Aotearoa, the Pacific, and the world. This is a rare opportunity to be part of a collaborative and dedicated HR team. We are looking for an experienced and confident HR generalist, who enjoys working independently to develop pragmatic HR solutions for clients, to join us. The role | Te mahi Working closely with the Divisional HR Manager and full-time HR Adviser, you will provide generalist HR advice and support to clients within the Operations Division. This will involve the provision of HR technical guidance and support on a range of matters including disputes resolution, restructuring and workplace change, personal grievances, management of conduct and capability, training and development, and effective implementation of employment agreements, policies, procedures and best practice. You will have the support of a comprehensive HR infrastructure, and colleagues both within the HR Advisory team and across the wider Human Resources Division. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for a professional who will champion our values: Manaakitaka/Respect, Pono/Integrity, Māhirahira/Curiosity and Whakawhanaukataka/Community. You will also have: •    Well-developed understanding of HR processes and policies. •    Several years of recent experience in a generalist HR role. •    Excellent knowledge of NZ employment legislation and its application. •    Strong interpersonal skills, including a level of comfort with complex or challenging situations. •    Dedication to working in a culturally safe and respectful manner, including understanding and appreciation of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles, and Pasifika values. You will be an effective communicator and facilitator, with proven success in building effective professional relationships and proven conflict resolutions skills. A Tertiary qualification in a relevant field is desirable. Further details | Pūroko This role is a part-time (22.5 hours per week), fixed term (12 months) position within the Operations Division, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment salary range for this role is $96,587 to $105,532. Top of range is $114,474, pro rata, per annum. We offer an amazing team environment, 5 weeks’ annual leave (pro rata), a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and excellent professional development opportunities. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence - please contact Ray Tobin, Divisional Human Resources Manager via the contact details below Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601380 will close on Tuesday, 22 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Ray Tobin Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Management Band 1 (Range of Appointments $96,587 to $105,532, Top of Range $114,474) Organisation HR Divisional Advice and Support Job Function HR Adviser ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601346": {
      "title": "Assistant Services Representative",
      "description": "Job Description - Assistant Services Representative - AskOtago (2601346) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Assistant Services Representative - AskOtago (2601346) Assistant Services Representative - AskOtago - 2601346 __________ DIVISIONAL SERVICES AND ADMINISTRATION | WHAKARATOKA ME NGĀ WHAKARITEKA Ā-WĀHAKA ASKOTAGO | UIAŌTĀKOU Looking for a part-time role that fits around your studies? Interested in technology and enjoy helping others? Join the AskOtago Student IT team and gain valuable work experience while studying at Otago. This role is ideal for university students who want to develop practical skills in customer service, communication, problem solving and IT support within a professional and supportive environment. These roles are available for the 2027 academic year, February to November. About the Team | Mō te Tīma AskOtago Student IT is part of AskOtago, the University of Otago's service centre, and provides frontline IT support to students, staff, and visitors. As one of the first points of contact for technology-related enquiries, our team helps customers access and use University systems, software, devices, and digital services. Our approach combines excellent customer service with practical technical support. We believe great IT support starts with understanding people's needs and helping them find the right solution. The Role | Te Mahi As an Assistant Service Representative (IT), you will provide frontline support for a wide range of technology-related enquiries. Working as part of a supportive and collaborative team, you will help customers across multiple service channels and contribute to a positive customer experience. Key responsibilities include: •    Providing friendly and professional IT support to students, staff, and visitors. •    Assisting with software, login, account, and device-related enquiries. •    Supporting customers to access and use University systems and digital services. •    Maintaining student computer and printing spaces across campus. •    Troubleshooting common technical issues and escalating more complex problems when required. •    Maintaining accurate records of customer interactions. •    Contributing to continuous improvement and service excellence. No specialist IT qualifications are required. We're looking for people with a genuine interest in technology and helping others. Full training will be provided. Your Skills and Experience | Kā Pūkeka me Kā Wheako We are looking for people who: •    Have excellent customer service and communication skills. •    Are enthusiastic, proactive, and keen to learn. •    Have an interest in technology and digital services. •    Enjoy problem-solving and working through challenges. •    Are confident learning new systems and applications. •    Can explain technical information in a clear and user-friendly way. •    Work effectively both independently and as part of a team. •    Take pride in delivering high-quality service. Previous experience in customer service, retail, hospitality, administration, or technical support will be viewed favourably but is not essential. Why join us? | He aha ai mātou? This role offers more than just a part-time job, you’ll gain: •    Part-time hours that fit around your study commitments and a handy on-campus location. •    Hands-on experience in a professional IT and customer service environment. •    Transferrable skills in communication, teamwork, problem solving and technology support. •    Experience that will strengthen your CV and support your future career goals. Whether you’re interested in technology, business, health sciences or another field entirely, this role provides valuable workplace experience that future employers are looking for. Further Details | Pūroko These are fixed term (February 2027 – November 2027), variable hour positions, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. There are multiple positions available. Successful applicants will be required to: •    Work between 10 and 20 hours per week during semester time. •    Be available to work additional hours during peak periods, including opportunities for full-time work during semester breaks and other non-teaching periods. The appointment range for this role is $59,494 to $63,000. Top of range $68,847 per year. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. For more information or to discuss this role in confidence, please contact Nick Anderson via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application, please provide a CV and cover letter outlining your interest in the role and the skills and experience you would bring to the team. Applications quoting reference 2601346 will close Tuesday, 22 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. #LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Nick Anderson Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 3 (Range of Appointments $59,494 to $63,000, Top of Range $68,847) Organisation AskOtago Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601257": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Applied Linguistics (TESOL)",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Applied Linguistics (TESOL) (2601257) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Applied Linguistics (TESOL) (2601257) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Applied Linguistics (TESOL) - 2601257 __________ TE REO INARIHI ME TE MĀTAI WETEREO | ENGLISH AND LINGUISTICS PROGRAMME TE PAE TAKATA | SCHOOL OF ARTS Mō mātou | About us Founded in 1869, the University of Otago is the oldest University in New Zealand. We like to think of the English and Linguistics Programme, however, as both the oldest and the newest with regard to the range of our teaching and research, with offerings from Medieval literature through to narrative fiction and poetry in digital media. We have creative writing offerings at all undergraduate levels, with papers the engage with fiction, poetry, creative non-fiction, travel writing, and creative writing for videogames and interactive media. Te mahi | The role The English and Linguistics Programme is inviting applications for a Lecturer/ Senior Lecturer position in Applied Linguistics/ TESOL. This position involves a combination of teaching, research, publication, and service. The role will be informed by Te Tiriti o Waitangi and an engagement with te reo, tikanga, and te ao Māori. The successful candidate will have knowledge and expertise in sociolinguistics and language education, including bilingualism and multilingualism, multilingual education, language-in-education policy, and TESOL curriculum and pedagogies that support linguistically diverse learners. They will have confidence and competency teaching in diverse cultural environments. Applicants with Aotearoa New Zealand teacher registration are strongly preferred. The successful candidate will coordinate, teach, and/or co-teach a range of our linguistics and TESOL undergraduate and postgraduate papers from 100- to 500-level. You will take a foundational role in building and supporting the Master of TESOL postgraduate qualification in coordination with the TESOL Coordinator. They will supervise research at Honours, MA, and PhD levels, while developing and maintaining a strong research profile of their own. They will seek external research funding, fulfill administrative roles, and undertake professional service activities that contribute to the overall effectiveness and standing of the programme, including community outreach through partnerships with schools and initiatives that support teacher professional learning. Kā pūkeke me kā wheako | Skills and Experience The successful candidate is expected to hold a PhD degree in Applied Linguistics/ TESOL. You will also have a proven track record of publications in Applied Linguistics/ TESOL, and the ability to teach across a wide range of undergraduate and postgraduate linguistics and TESOL papers and supervise postgraduate research. Ideal candidates will also possess the following skills and experience: •    A commitment to being a productive collaborator with a track record of working collegially. •    An excellent research and publication profile. •    Demonstrated experience and ability in delivering high-quality teaching. •    Demonstrated experience in supporting the professional learning and development of pre-service and in-service language teachers, preferably within Aotearoa New Zealand. •    A commitment to fostering collaborative teaching and research relationships with Māori and/or Pasifika communities, and demonstrated multi-cultural competence in teaching. •    A commitment to a curriculum that includes gender-diverse, racially diverse, and neuro-diverse authors and topics in their teaching. Pūroko | Further Information This is a permanent, full-time position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, and is available from February 2027. The appointment ranges for this role are: •    Lecturer - $97,377 to $ 114,943 per annum. •    Senior Lecturer - $ 120,494 to $137,444 per annum. Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Professor David Ciccoricco, Tumuaki - Head of Programme, English & Linguistics via the contact details below. Tono | Application Candidates are required to submit the following: •    A CV, including education and employment history. •    A letter of application outlining how you fit the role (max. 2 pages). •    3–4 referee contact details, including telephone number and email address. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601257 will close on 11:59pm (NZST) on Monday, 5 October 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor David Ciccoricco Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) or Senior Lecturer / Senior Research Fellow ($120,494 to $137,444) Organisation English and Linguistics Job Function Academic ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601312": {
      "title": "Senior Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine  (2601312) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine  (2601312) Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine - 2601312 __________ DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGICAL MEDICINE, DUNEDIN | TE TARI WHAKAORA HINENGARO TANGATA DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI The Role | Te mahi The Department of Psychological Medicine (Dunedin) advances psychological medicine through the integration of academic and clinical expertise in psychiatry, clinical psychology, and health psychology. We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to contribute to teaching within the MB ChB programme, and the Royal Australian and New Zealand College’s Formal Education Course, Registrar programme, in 2027. This position will play an important role in supporting the delivery and ongoing development of the curriculum, helping to equip future medical graduates with the knowledge, skills, and professional capabilities required to work effectively in the field of psychological medicine. The role includes convening responsibilities for the Year 6 programme and the Registrar Teaching programme, along with supporting teaching, assessment, and curriculum initiatives across the Department. Key responsibilities include: •    Convening responsibilities •    Supporting the development and delivery of course materials. •    Assisting with the administration and coordination of the MB ChB programme and Registrar programme. •    Collaborating with academic teaching and research staff •    Supporting strategic projects and initiatives that enhance teaching and curriculum development. •    Contributing to assessment activities and associated administration. •    Assisting with teaching-related operational and documentation requirements. Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako To be successful in this role, you will have: •    A medical qualification (eligible to work in NZ) as a Consultant Psychiatrist. We would consider Senior Psychiatric Registrars who are close to completing their fellowship. •    Teaching experience in the field of psychological medicine (preferable) •    Strong organisational and administrative skills. •    The ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively. •    A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Further Details | Pūroko The Department is seeking to fill a total of 0.4 FTE (15 hours per week) and is open to appointing either: •    One candidate at 0.4 FTE, or •    Two candidates at 0.2 FTE (7.5 hours per week each). This is a fixed-term position from the 7th of December 2026 for the Registrar convening role, or the 18 January 2027, for the MB ChB Year 6 convening role, to 28 January 2028, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The salary for this position will be based on the successful candidate's skills, experience, and qualifications and is expected to fall within the range of Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow Practice Fellow - $109,554 to $120,528 or $148,414 to $184,993 (pro rata). The University of Otago is a workplace that values and celebrates diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. We are committed to fostering an environment where staff from a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives feel valued, respected, and supported to succeed. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Confidential enquiries regarding the role may be directed to Associate Professor Tess Patterson via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application, please click the apply button and include your CV and cover letter. Applications quoting reference number 2601312 will close on Sunday, 18 October 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Associate Professor Tess Patterson Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow - Medical & Dental 3 ($109,554 to $120,528) or 4 ($148,414 to $184,993) Organisation Psychological Medicine Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601373": {
      "title": "Senior Professional Practice Fellow/Lecturer/Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow/Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - College of Education (2601373) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow/Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - College of Education (2601373) Senior Professional Practice Fellow/Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - College of Education - 2601373 __________ TE KETE ARONUI/DIVISION OF HUMANITIES TE KURA ĀKAU TAITOKA/COLLEGE OF EDUCATION Te Kura Ākau Taitoka/The University of Otago College of Education is dedicated to the enhancement of educational practice through high-quality teaching, scholarship, research, professional leadership and community service, to benefit mokopuna, ākoka, kaiako, whānau and the wider community (in local, national and international settings). Te Kura Akau Taitoka is committed to teaching and learning practices that honour indigenous language, culture, and knowledge systems and promote equitable and successful educational outcomes for Māori and Pacific learners, their whānau, families, and communities. We especially value our relationship with Kāi Tahu, who hold mana whenua status within our takiwā of Te Waipounamu, and who are the University’s principal Tiriti partner. Te mahi/The role We are looking to appoint two outstanding permanent staff: a full-time Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua (Lecturer/Senior Lecturer) with research expertise and specialist expertise in mathematics education AND a full-time Tauira Mātāmua Riteka Kaio (Senior Professional Practice Fellow) with expertise across the primary curriculum. The successful candidates will be able to teach across a range of curriculum areas, and to contribute to teaching across our Initial Teacher Education and Education Studies programmes. The successful candidates will have a demonstrated commitment to culturally sustaining practice. Expertise in Pacific education and/or proficiency in te reo Māori is highly desirable. The successful candidates will teach undergraduate and postgraduate students in Initial Teacher Education and Education Studies papers. If appointed as a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer, you will also supervise postgraduate research students and develop your own programme of research. If you are a New Zealand registered teacher, you may also visit Initial Teacher Education students on professional experience. These positions provide the opportunity to work within a collegial department, committed to upholding the articles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi, and dedicated to excellence in teacher education and educational research. The exact nature of the appointments will depend on the successful candidates’ skills and experience. Kā Pūkeka me kā Wheako/Skills and Experience The successful candidates will be expected to have: •    Specialist expertise in mathematics education and/or primary curriculum. •    Demonstrated ability to contribute to teaching across a range of curriculum areas within Initial Teacher Education and Education Studies programmes. •    Understanding of and experience in the New Zealand education sector. •    Evidence of an ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds. •    Demonstrated commitment to the articles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi, and to equal opportunity. •    A commitment to developing collaborative working relationships with colleagues and to supporting the University’s Māori, Pacific, and Sustainability strategic frameworks. •    Expertise in Pacific education and/or proficiency in te reo Māori (desirable). Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua (Lecturer/Senior Lecturer) As a Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua (Lecturer/Senior Lecturer), you will be expected to actively engage in research. You will also be actively involved in the recruitment of new research students and applying for and receiving external research funding. If appointed as a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer, to be successful in this role you will have: •    A PhD degree. •    Demonstrated teaching experience at tertiary level. •    Relevant research experience and a corresponding publication record. •    New Zealand teacher registration and a current full practising certificate are desirable. Tauira Mātāmua Riteka Kaio (Senior Professional Practice Fellow) If appointed as a Tauira Mātāmua Riteka Kaio (Senior Professional Practice Fellow), you will be expected to contribute to our programmes in ways that reinforce and promote inter-dependence between teaching, research and professional practice. If appointed as a Senior Professional Practice Fellow, to be successful in this role you will have: •    A Master’s degree or postgraduate qualification (desirable). •    A relevant teaching qualification and New Zealand teacher registration with a current full practising certificate. •    Relevant professional experience in a primary school setting. We particularly welcome applications from candidates with experience supporting students to develop the knowledge and skills to work in culturally sustaining ways with Māori and Pacific communities. Pūroko/Further Details We have two full-time, permanent positions Ōtepoti, Dunedin. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Specific enquiries regarding the role may be directed to Professor Vivienne Anderson, Manutaki/Dean of Te Kura Ākau Taitoka/the College of Education, University of Otago, via the contact details below. Tono/Application Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601373 will close on Sunday 27 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor Vivienne Anderson Position details: Information Statement - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Position details: Senior Professional Practice Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation College of Education Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601264": {
      "title": "College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027",
      "description": "Job Description - College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 (2601264) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 (2601264) College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 - 2601264 __________ Applications are invited for the College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 Applications are invited for the 2027 residency from children’s writers who usually reside in Aotearoa New Zealand. Begun by the Dunedin College of Education in 1992, the College of Education Children’s Writer in Residence, has allowed children’s writers to write full time in a compatible environment among colleagues who are involved with the teaching of reading and literature to children.  The financial support and imaginative space the residency afford has enabled writers to create a huge variety and number of books for New Zealand’s young readers. With support from Creative New Zealand to fund the writer’s remuneration, the residency is offered in partnership with the Robert Lord Writers Cottage Trust, which provides rent-free accommodation to writers in the historic Titan Street cottage bequeathed by the late playwright Robert Lord. What the Residency offers •    A 14-week full-time position (February 2027). •    A stipend equivalent to a University Lecturer’s salary (pro-rata), less the value of accommodation provided at the Robert Lord Writers Cottage. •    Furnished accommodation, including wireless broadband internet access at the Robert Lord Writers Cottage.  Excludes power costs. •    A dedicated writing office on campus within College of Education. •    Access to the University Library and wider University community. Who can apply Writers who are normally resident in New Zealand. A degree or formal qualification is not required. But a serious commitment to the craft needs to be demonstrated through published work or its equivalent. Early  and mid career writers are encouraged to apply. What to include Applications must include: •    Covering letter including short biography. •    Curriculum vitae. •    Details of published work & any writing awards. •    A 500-word outline of work contemplated. •    Samples of work (500 words maximum). PDF or Word files are preferred. •    Two referee reports, from referees well placed to comment on your writing experience (view the template HERE ). How to apply Applications quoting reference number 2601264 will close on Sunday, 18 October 2026. Please find links below for Conditions of Award and further information about the Residency. Additional Information Contact: Arts Fellowship Administrator Position details: Conditions of Award Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Humanities Divisional Office Job Function Fellowships ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    }
  },
